WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.240
IT HAS BEEN ALMOST ONE YEAR SINCE CONGESTION&nbsp;
PRICING OFFICIALLY WENT INTO EFFECT IN NEW&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.400
YORK CITY. ON JANUARY 5TH, THE CITY BECAME THE&nbsp;
FIRST IN THE NATION TO CHARGE MOST DRIVERS A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.520
DAILY FEE TO ENTER MANHATTAN SOUTH OF 60TH&nbsp;
STREET. SUPPORTERS SAID IT WOULD HELP CLEAR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.680
UP TRAFFIC, IMPROVE AIR QUALITY, AND RAISE&nbsp;
BILLIONS FOR LONG-OVERDUE MTA UPGRADES.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.040
BUT CRITICS WARNED IT COULD&nbsp;
HURT WORKING-CLASS COMMUTERS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.800
PUSH POLLUTION INTO OUTER-BOROUGH COMMUNITIES,&nbsp;
AND CREATE NEW INEQUITIES ACROSS THE CITY.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.400
BEFORE THE PROGRAM LAUNCHED, I SPOKE&nbsp;
WITH THE THEN COMMUNICATIONS MANAGER&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.345
AT TRANSPORTATION ALTERNATIVES, ONE OF THE&nbsp;
GROUPS THAT ADVOCATED FOR CONGESTION PRICING.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.800
JACOB DECASTRO:
a cost that we don't often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.440
talk about, too is the cost of congestion.&nbsp;
In New York City, which has been, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.280
regarded as having the worst traffic congestion&nbsp;
in the whole world. So that's, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.560
lost productivity. You know, people who are riding&nbsp;
the bus are stuck in traffic on these congested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.880
streets across the city. That's a that's a toll&nbsp;
on people who are using transit to get around.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.000
THE CORE IDEA BEHIND CONGESTION PRICING IS SIMPLE:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.105
FEWER CARS ENTERING MANHATTAN MEANS LESS&nbsp;
GRIDLOCK, FASTER BUSES, AND CLEANER AIR.

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:14.320
JACOB DECASTRO:
the money that is going to be raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.720
from congestion pricing is going to go towards&nbsp;
making subway commutes more reliable. Is it going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.640
to make sure that people have an elevator to get&nbsp;
out of the station? You know, increasing access,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.840
increasing accessibility On our transit&nbsp;
system. And for people that do choose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.600
still drive into the into the central business&nbsp;
district. There's going to be less traffic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.920
for them too so they can get around faster.&nbsp;
They can get where they're going faster, too.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.800
BUT AT A COST OF NINE DOLLARS DURING PEAK&nbsp;
HOURS FOR THE AVERAGE PASSENGER VEHICLE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.680
CRITICS ARGUE THE PROGRAM ADDS ANOTHER&nbsp;
FINANCIAL BURDEN — ESPECIALLY FOR PEOPLE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.520
WHO HAVE NO CHOICE BUT TO DRIVE. OTHERS WORRY&nbsp;
ABOUT HOW THE POLICY AFFECTS NEIGHBORHOODS FAR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.680
FROM MIDTOWN. COMMUNITIES IN THE SOUTH BRONX HAVE&nbsp;
SOME OF THE HIGHEST ASTHMA RATES IN THE COUNTRY.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.120
ADVOCATES THERE FEAR THAT DRIVERS AVOIDING&nbsp;
TOLLS COULD DIVERT INTO THEIR NEIGHBORHOODS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.080
PUSHING MORE TRUCK TRAFFIC ONTO ALREADY&nbsp;
POLLUTED CORRIDORS LIKE THE CROSS BRONX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.920
EXPRESSWAY. BUT THOSE IN FAVOR OF CONGESTION&nbsp;
PRICING SAY THOSE CONCERNS ARE BEING ADDRESSED.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.160
JACOB DECASTRO:

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.960
one of the things that the MTA was planning&nbsp;
on doing as part of the environmental review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.560
was dedicating hundreds of millions of dollars to&nbsp;
air quality mitigation measures in the Bronx. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.520
things like a, like all electric busses, are going&nbsp;
to be prioritized for the Bronx. there's a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.000
other programs that were dependent on congestion&nbsp;
pricing starting to mitigate those air quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.600
concerns. And that's not to say there more work&nbsp;
has to be done, because more work absolutely has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:45.600
to be done. But without congestion pricing, those&nbsp;
air quality improvement measures are at risk, too.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.520
NEARLY A YEAR INTO THE PROGRAM, THE MTA IS&nbsp;
CALLING IT A SUCCESS. OFFICIALS SAY ABOUT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.240
60,000 FEWER VEHICLES ARE ENTERING&nbsp;
THE CONGESTION RELIEF ZONE EACH DAY,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.360
AND THE PROGRAM GENERATED 48 MILLION&nbsp;
DOLLARS IN ITS FIRST MONTH ALONE.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.240
BUT THE CONCERNS HAVEN’T DISAPPEARED. SOME&nbsp;
DRIVERS SAY THE FINANCIAL STRAIN IS REAL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.400
ENVIRONMENTAL GROUPS IN THE BRONX ARE&nbsp;
STILL CLOSELY MONITORING TRUCK TRAFFIC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:16.240
AND QUESTIONS ABOUT FAIRNESS — WHO PAYS AND WHO&nbsp;
BENEFITS — CONTINUE TO SHAPE PUBLIC OPINION.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.640
THE PROGRAM ALSO FACES ONGOING LEGAL&nbsp;
UNCERTAINTY. THE TRUMP ADMINISTRATION&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.200
HAS THREATENED FEDERAL ACTION TO SHUT IT DOWN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.960
AND THE FUTURE OF CONGESTION PRICING REMAINS&nbsp;
TIED UP IN COURT AS THE CASE MOVES FORWARD.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.840
SUPPORTERS POINT TO OTHER&nbsp;
MAJOR CITIES, LIKE LONDON,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.040
AS EVIDENCE THAT CONGESTION&nbsp;
PRICING CAN WORK LONG-TERM.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.080
JACOB DECASTRO:
they've had a congestion pricing program for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.640
you know, almost two decades now that has reduced&nbsp;
traffic. That has led to increases, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.440
in public transit usage, has provided funding for&nbsp;
for transit upgrades, and has made streets safer.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:57.200
AS NEW YORK APPROACHES THE ONE-YEAR ANNIVERSARY OF&nbsp;
CONGESTION PRICING, THE DEBATE IS FAR FROM OVER.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.800
WHAT IS CLEAR IS THAT THIS POLICY HAS ALREADY&nbsp;
RESHAPED HOW THE CITY MOVES — AND THE EFFECTS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.880
BOTH POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE, WILL&nbsp;
CONTINUE TO UNFOLD LONG AFTER YEAR ONE.
